"""### 4. LSTM Model

#### Data Preparation for LSTM

LSTMs are sensitive to the scale of input data, so we'll standardize the features. Additionally, LSTM layers expect a 3D input tensor with the shape `(samples, timesteps, features)`. Since we're working with daily data and predicting the next day's flow based on previous features, we'll treat each sample as a single timestep with multiple features.
"""

from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler

# Initialize the scaler
scaler = StandardScaler()

# Fit the scaler on the stochastically partitioned training features and transform both sets
X_train_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)
X_test_scaled = scaler.transform(X_test)

print("Shape of X_train_scaled before reshaping:", X_train_scaled.shape)
print("Shape of X_test_scaled before reshaping:", X_test_scaled.shape)

# Reshape input to be 3D [samples, timesteps, features] for the LSTM model
